  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":23,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A theatre has 24 rows of seats. The first row has 18 seats and each row has 2 more seats than the row in front of it.\n\n(a) State the number of seats in the 10th row.\n(b) Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.\n(c) A renovation adds one extra seat to every row. Explain how the total changes.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A theatre has 24 rows of seats. The first row has 18 seats and each row has 2 more seats than the row in front of it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the number of seats in the 10th row.\n(b) Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.\n(c) A renovation adds one extra seat to every row. Explain how the total changes."}]}]},"e":"u_10 = 36, u_24 = 64, so S_24 = 12 × 82 = 984 seats. Adding one seat per row adds 24 seats.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"arithmetic sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":23,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"For an arithmetic series find the first and last terms, then apply S_n = n/2 (u_1 + u_n).","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the number of seats in the 10th row.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The first row has 18 seats and each row adds 2.","u_n = 18 + 2(n − 1), so use n = 10."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":36,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the number of seats in the last (24th) row.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use n = 24 in the nth-term formula.","18 + 2(23)."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":64,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["S_n = n/2 × (first + last).","24/2 × (18 + 64)."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":984,"tolerance":0,"unit":"seats"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain how the total changes when one seat is added to every row.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["How many rows are there?","Each row gains exactly one seat."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["the total rises by 24 seats, one for each of the 24 rows, giving 1008"],"keywords":[["total"],["rises"],["seats"],["rows"],["giving"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for substituting n = 24, 1 for 64. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 82, 1 for 984. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 24 seats, 1 for 1 008.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for substituting n = 24, 1 for 64. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 82, 1 for 984. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 24 seats, 1 for 1 008.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write the nth-term formula explicitly in (a); the same formula drives (b) and (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for substituting n = 24, 1 for 64. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 82, 1 for 984. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 24 seats, 1 for 1 008."},{"n":24,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A culture of bacteria doubles every 3 hours. It starts with 500 bacteria.\n\n(a) State the number of bacteria after 12 hours.\n(b) Explain why the number after t hours is not a linear function of t.\n(c) Calculate the number of complete 3-hour periods needed for the population to exceed 50 000.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A culture of bacteria doubles every 3 hours. It starts with 500 bacteria."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the number of bacteria after 12 hours.\n(b) Explain why the number after t hours is not a linear function of t.\n(c) Calculate the number of complete 3-hour periods needed for the population to exceed 50 000."}]}]},"e":"Four periods give 500 × 16 = 8 000 bacteria. The growth is 500 × 2^(t/3), a geometric model. 2^k > 100 first holds at k = 7 (128), so 7 periods are needed.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"geometric sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":24,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Doubling is multiplication: count periods, then use a power. Compare with linear growth explicitly.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the number of doubling periods in 12 hours.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The population doubles every 3 hours.","Divide 12 by 3."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the number of bacteria after 12 hours.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply the starting 500 by 2 once for each period.","500 × 2^4."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":8000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"bacteria"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the population is not a linear function of time.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare what happens in successive 3-hour periods.","Is a constant amount added each period, or a constant factor?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["each period multiplies the population by 2 rather than adding a fixed number, so the growth is geometric (exponential), not linear"],"keywords":[["period"],["multiplies"],["population"],["rather"],["adding"],["fixed"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the number of complete periods needed to exceed 50 000 bacteria.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["You need 500 × 2^k > 50 000, so 2^k > 100.","2^6 = 64 and 2^7 = 128, so take the first k that works."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"periods"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^4, 1 for 8 000. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''multiply by 2'', 1 for the linear contrast. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the inequality 2^k > 100, 1 for testing powers, 1 for 7.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^4, 1 for 8 000. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''multiply by 2'', 1 for the linear contrast. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the inequality 2^k > 100, 1 for testing powers, 1 for 7.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the population against the period number in the workspace: the curve (not a straight line) is the evidence for (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^4, 1 for 8 000. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''multiply by 2'', 1 for the linear contrast. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the inequality 2^k > 100, 1 for testing powers, 1 for 7."},{"n":25,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Amara invests $5 000 at 4% per year compound interest. Her friend Ben invests $5 000 at 4% per year simple interest.\n\n(a) Calculate the value of Amara''s investment after 5 years.\n(b) Calculate the value of Ben''s investment after 5 years.\n(c) Explain why the difference grows each year.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Amara invests "},{"type":"text","text":"$5 000 at 4% per year compound interest. Her friend Ben invests $"},{"type":"text","text":"5 000 at 4% per year simple interest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the value of Amara''s investment after 5 years.\n(b) Calculate the value of Ben''s investment after 5 years.\n(c) Explain why the difference grows each year."}]}]},"e":"Amara: 5000(1.04)^5 = $6 083.26. Ben: 5000 + 5(200) = $6 000. The $83.26 gap exists because Amara''s interest compounds, so each year''s interest is calculated on a larger balance.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"compound interest","st":"compound interest","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":25,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Compound interest is geometric; simple interest is arithmetic. State the base each year''s interest is calculated on.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the value of Amara''s investment after 5 years, to the nearest cent.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use A = P(1 + r)^n with r = 0.04 and n = 5.","5000 × 1.04^5."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":6083.26,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the value of Ben''s investment after 5 years.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Simple interest adds 4% of $5 000 each year.","5000 + 5 × 200."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":6000,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the gap between the two investments widens each year.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What is each person''s interest calculated on?","Does Ben''s interest ever earn interest?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["amara earns interest on the interest she has already received, so her balance grows by an increasing amount, while ben always earns 4% of the original $5000"],"keywords":[["amara"],["earns"],["interest"],["already"],["received"],["balance"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 1.04^5, 1 for $6 083.26. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5 × 200, 1 for $6 000. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''interest on interest'', 1 for the constant simple base, 1 for the widening gap.","marking_criteria":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 1.04^5, 1 for $6 083.26. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5 × 200, 1 for $6 000. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''interest on interest'', 1 for the constant simple base, 1 for the widening gap.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show P(1 + r)^n with the numbers substituted; the exponent is the point being assessed."},"marking":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 1.04^5, 1 for $6 083.26. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5 × 200, 1 for $6 000. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''interest on interest'', 1 for the constant simple base, 1 for the widening gap."},{"n":26,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A ball is dropped from 2 m. After each bounce it rises to 60% of its previous height.\n\n(a) State the height after the first bounce.\n(b) Calculate the height after the third bounce.\n(c) Calculate the total vertical distance travelled before the ball comes to rest.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A ball is dropped from 2 m. After each bounce it rises to 60% of its previous height."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the height after the first bounce.\n(b) Calculate the height after the third bounce.\n(c) Calculate the total vertical distance travelled before the ball comes to rest."}]}]},"e":"Bounce heights 1.2, 0.72, 0.432, … have sum 1.2/0.4 = 3 m. The ball travels that sum up and down, so 2 × 3 = 6 m of bounces, plus the initial 2 m fall: 8 m in total.","m":10,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"limits of series","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In a bouncing-ball problem, list the bounce heights as a geometric sequence and remember each bounce is travelled twice.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the height after the first bounce, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["60% of the 2 m drop.","2 × 0.6."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1.2,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"m"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the height after the third bounce, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The heights form a geometric sequence with r = 0.6.","2 × 0.6^3."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":0.432,"tolerance":0.002,"unit":"m"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the bounce distances are multiplied by 2 in the total distance.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Does the ball only go up, or up and then down?","Each bounce contributes a rise and an equal fall."],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["each bounce is travelled going up and again coming down, so the bounce sum is counted twice, plus the original 2 m drop"],"keywords":[["bounce"],["travelled"],["going"],["again"],["coming"],["down"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the total vertical distance before the ball comes to rest, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":4,"hints":["Sum the bounce heights with S∞ = a/(1 − r).","Add the original 2 m fall and double the bounce sum."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":8,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"m"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the ratio, 1 for 0.432. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''up and down'', 1 for the doubling, 1 for including the first drop. (d) 4 marks: 1 for S∞, 1 for 3 m, 1 for 6 m, 1 for 8 m.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the ratio, 1 for 0.432. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''up and down'', 1 for the doubling, 1 for including the first drop. (d) 4 marks: 1 for S∞, 1 for 3 m, 1 for 6 m, 1 for 8 m.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write the bounce heights as a sequence before summing; the doubling in (c) is where most marks are lost."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the ratio, 1 for 0.432. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''up and down'', 1 for the doubling, 1 for including the first drop. (d) 4 marks: 1 for S∞, 1 for 3 m, 1 for 6 m, 1 for 8 m."},{"n":27,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The 2nd term of a geometric sequence is 12 and the 5th term is 96.\n\n(a) Explain how to find the common ratio from these two terms.\n(b) Calculate the common ratio.\n(c) Calculate the first term.\n(d) State the 7th term.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 2nd term of a geometric sequence is 12 and the 5th term is 96."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Explain how to find the common ratio from these two terms.\n(b) Calculate the common ratio.\n(c) Calculate the first term.\n(d) State the 7th term."}]}]},"e":"u_5/u_2 = r³ = 8 gives r = 2, so u_1 = 12/2 = 6 and u_7 = 6 × 2^6 = 384.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"geometric sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Dividing two terms of a geometric sequence gives r raised to the difference in their positions.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Explain how the two terms give the common ratio.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Write u_2 and u_5 in terms of u_1 and r.","How many factors of r separate the 2nd and 5th terms?"],"strand":"C(i)","accepted":["dividing u_5 by u_2 removes the first term and leaves r^3, because the terms are three positions apart"],"keywords":[["dividing"],["removes"],["first"],["term"],["leaves"],["terms"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the common ratio.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["96 ÷ 12 = 8, and that equals r³.","Take the cube root of 8."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":2,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the first term.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["u_2 = u_1 × r.","12 = 2 × u_1."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":6,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the 7th term.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The 7th term is six multiplications of r after the first term.","u_7 = 6 × 2^6."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":384,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for ''three positions, so r cubed''. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 8, 1 for r = 2. (c) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 6. (d) 2 marks: 1 for r^6, 1 for 384.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for ''three positions, so r cubed''. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 8, 1 for r = 2. (c) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 6. (d) 2 marks: 1 for r^6, 1 for 384.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (a) use the word ''divide'' and the difference in position (3 steps); that is the reasoning being marked."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for ''three positions, so r cubed''. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 8, 1 for r = 2. (c) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 6. (d) 2 marks: 1 for r^6, 1 for 384."},{"n":28,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A savings plan pays $200 in month 1, $220 in month 2, $240 in month 3, and so on, increasing by $20 each month.\n\n(a) State the amount paid in month 12.\n(b) Calculate the total saved after 12 months.\n(c) Explain how many months are needed for the total to exceed $5 000, setting up the inequality you would solve.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A savings plan pays "},{"type":"text","text":"$200 in month 1, $"},{"type":"text","text":"220 in month 2, "},{"type":"text","text":"$240 in month 3, and so on, increasing by $"},{"type":"text","text":"20 each month."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the amount paid in month 12.\n(b) Calculate the total saved after 12 months.\n(c) Explain how many months are needed for the total to exceed "},{"type":"text","text":"$5 000, setting up the inequality you would solve."}]}]},"e":"Month 12 is 200 + 11(20) = $420 and S_12 = 6(620) = $3 720. S_14 = 7(200 + 460) = $4 620, which is still below 5000, while S_15 = 7.5(200 + 480) = $5 100 exceeds it, so 15 months are needed.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"sigma notation","st":"sigma notation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For ''when does the total exceed X'', compute running sums and bracket the answer between two months rather than guessing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the amount paid in month 12, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The amounts form an arithmetic sequence with d = 20.","200 + 11 × 20."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":420,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total saved after 12 months, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["S_n = n/2 × (first + last).","12/2 × (200 + 420)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":3720,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the total saved after 14 months, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use the formula with n = 14 and last term 200 + 13(20).","14/2 × (200 + 460)."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":4620,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the first month for which the total exceeds $5 000.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Compare your 14-month total with 5000; it is still short.","Add one more month''s payment to your 14-month total and compare again."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":15,"tolerance":0,"unit":"months"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for the last term, 1 for $3 720. (c) 3 marks: 1 for n = 14, 1 for the last term 460, 1 for $4 620. (d) 2 marks: 1 for comparing, 1 for 15 months.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for the last term, 1 for $3 720. (c) 3 marks: 1 for n = 14, 1 for the last term 460, 1 for $4 620. (d) 2 marks: 1 for comparing, 1 for 15 months.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Build the monthly totals in the workspace table; the sequence of running totals answers (d) directly."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for the last term, 1 for $3 720. (c) 3 marks: 1 for n = 14, 1 for the last term 460, 1 for $4 620. (d) 2 marks: 1 for comparing, 1 for 15 months."}]'::jsonb) as x
